{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>I removed</b> — I am the one who removed the burden from the shoulder of Israel.</p>" "<b>and his hands passed</b> — they departed from making clay, from the work of clay and bricks, and from carrying them in baskets. And it is possible: since <b>a lip I did not know</b> is an allusion to Israel, who heard the voice of HaShem at Sinai — and behold <b>a lip</b> is construct, for Israel did not know HaShem properly until after they heard His voice, and so it is written: <i>You were shown to know</i> [Deut. 4:35] and <i>from the heavens He made you hear His voice</i> [Deut. 4:36]. Or <b>a lip I did not know</b> may be Moses, who was <i>uncircumcised of lips</i> [Exod. 6:12], and Aaron was his mouth, and Moses would speak the Name of Glory to Israel."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 81, :notes {1 "Reading מעשות חומר מעשות החומר as a dittography or appositional repetition; translated as a single phrase 'from making clay, from the work of clay.' The phrase דוד appears to mean baskets/panniers (Aramaic דודא), not the proper name David."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:35:43.803863388Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}